ith something? But there is dependency: reduction needs full colPref, so barrier needed.\n\nMaybe we can combine both loops into one collapsed loop over r and c only, not compute column prefixes in parallel separately? Actually the prefix sums are independent per column; could be computed inside same parallel region before reduction. Current code does that with implicit barrier. That's fine.\n\nWe need propose a new transformation. Since role C focuses on work sharing/scheduling/granularity, we can try using schedule(guided) for collapse(2)? But previous guided attempt (65eed1e01a6d466c duplicate of 29052a43635845be) had time ~0.058s, much slower. So not good.\n\nMaybe use static with chunk size equal to number of threads? Or use collapse(2) but with schedule(static, 4)? Could improve cache locality? Not sure.\n\nAnother angle: The current code uses vector<vector<int>> matrix; that's non-contiguous rows (each row separate allocation). Accessing column-wise in prefix computation causes poor cache behavior because matrix[i][j] strides across rows. Better to store matrix flat as well, or transpose? We can change input reading into a flat array and compute column prefixes more efficiently by iterating over columns but accessing contiguous memory if we store transposed? Actually for column prefix sums, we need sum down each column; if matrix is row-major, accessing matrix[i][j] with i varying causes stride n. That's cache-unfriendly. We could instead read input into a flat vector<int> mat(n*n) in row-major, then compute colPref by iterating over columns but still strided access. Alternatively, we can store the matrix transposed (column-major) to make column prefix contiguous. But reading input is given row-wise; we can fill both? Or we can compute column prefixes using a different algorithm: for each i from 0..n-1, update colPref[i*n+j] = colPref[(i-1)*n+j] + mat[i*n+j]; that's still strided but maybe compiler vectorizes? Actually if we loop over j inner and i outer, access to mat row contiguous; colPref also row-major so for fixed i, j varies contiguously. That would be better: compute prefix sums by rows rather than columns. Let's think.\n\nCurrent code:\n#pragma omp for schedule(static)\nfor(int j=0;j<n;++j) {\n    long long running = 0;\n    for(int i=0;i<n;++i) {\n        running += matrix[i][j];\n        colPref[i*n + j] = running;\n    }\n}\nThis loops over columns outer, rows inner; each thread handles a set of columns. For each column, it accesses matrix[i][j] with varying i -> stride n (bad). Better to loop over rows outer and update all columns:\n#pragma omp for schedule(static)\nfor(int i=0;i<n;++i) {\n    long long running = 0? No, need per-column running. We can maintain an array of running sums per column, but that's shared. Alternatively compute colPref row by row: colPref[i*n+j] = (i==0 ? mat[j] : colPref[(i-1)*n+j] + mat[i*n+j]). This accesses mat row contiguous and colPref previous row contiguous; good cache behavior. But parallelizing over rows would require each thread to process a set of rows, but there is dependency across rows for prefix sums (each row depends on previous). So cannot parallelize directly without restructuring.\n\nWe could compute column prefixes using two passes? Or use a parallel scan algorithm per column? That's more complex.\n\nAlternatively, we can store matrix transposed: read input into matT[j*n+i] = value. Then column j is contiguous in memory (matT + j*n). Then prefix sums over i are contiguous. But reading input row-wise would require writing to strided locations; that's okay because input reading is sequential and not performance critical? Actually reading N^2 ints from stdin may be significant but likely less than computation. We can read into a flat vector in row-major, then transpose? That adds O(N^2) work.\n\nMaybe we can avoid matrix entirely: compute column prefix sums on the fly while reading input? Since input is given row by row, we could accumulate running sums per column